Context
The CMS stores sensitive personal data. SQL injection is a critical security risk, and queries must handle tenant and user parameters safely.

Decision
Use parameterised SQL statements for all database operations within the repository layer. Manual string concatenation is forbidden.

Rationale
Eliminates SQL injection vulnerabilities
Ensures tenant filtering is applied correctly
Supports prepared statements for performance
Aligns with security best practices (OWASP)

Consequences
Repository layer must define strong typed interfaces
Developers need to avoid dynamic SQL generation
Slightly more verbose code, but safer

Alternatives Considered
ORM → too heavy for project scope, added complexity
Unparameterised raw SQL → unacceptable security risk
